WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:20.910
Sigmund Freud: the father of psychoanalysis
At the dawn of the 20th Century, one young

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:25.660
doctor in Vienna developed a theory that would
change our understanding of the human mind

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:26.660
forever.

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:31.619
Using cutting edge techniques the doctor delved
further into the psyche than anyone had before.

00:00:31.619 --> 00:00:36.399
He created names, like the Id and Superego,
that are still in use today.

00:00:36.399 --> 00:00:40.780
Catalogued patterns of thinking, like transference
and the Oedipus Complex, that still influence

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:42.600
us over a century later.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:49.250
Even now, his name is still instantly recognizable:
Sigmund Freud.

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:53.549
Born in the mid-19th Century in what was then
the Austrian Empire, Freud’s life was that

00:00:53.549 --> 00:00:55.449
of a consummate outsider.

00:00:55.449 --> 00:01:00.949
He was an ethnic Jew in a city of Catholics;
an atheist in community of practicing Jews.

00:01:00.949 --> 00:01:06.420
In a time of intense conservatism, he preached
that everyone was bisexual, and shocked society

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:10.920
when he suggested erotic desire was rooted
in childhood trauma.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:15.890
The father of psychoanalysis, the inspiration
for surrealism, and a maverick who courted

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:19.610
controversy, Freud ushered in our modern notion
of the mind.

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:27.189
Yet he died in exile, persecuted and loathed
by the city he’d dedicated his life to.

00:01:27.189 --> 00:01:33.950
Oedipus Freud
If you were to leave Vienna and walk due north

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:38.060
for 150 miles, you would eventually come to
a little town called Příbor.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:42.920
A small, somewhat pretty place, Příbor is
utterly unremarkable among Czech towns in

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:44.440
almost every respect.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:46.560
With one exception.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.649
It was here, on May 6, 1856, that Sigmund
Freud was born.

00:01:50.649 --> 00:01:55.399
At that time, Příbor was a heavily Catholic
town in the Austrian Empire with the German

00:01:55.399 --> 00:02:01.950
name of Freiberg, and a Jewish community so
tiny you could count its members on one hand.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:04.229
But small didn’t mean persecuted.

00:02:04.229 --> 00:02:07.869
Freud managed to be born at a fortuitous time
for Jews in the Austrian Empire.

00:02:07.869 --> 00:02:12.500
Just a few years before, the new emperor,
Franz Josef, had decreed the law would stop

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.260
persecuting other religions.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:18.860
No longer would the Empire’s Jewish inhabitants
be subject to ghettos and restrictions.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:23.290
They would be free, even to live unrestricted
in the imperial capital of Vienna.

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:26.490
Not that the Freuds immediately took advantage
of this right.

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:30.739
The first few years of Freud’s life were
passed in the pleasant dullness of Příbor,

00:02:30.739 --> 00:02:34.099
a dullness that nonetheless masked an eccentric
upbringing.

00:02:34.099 --> 00:02:38.939
Freud’s father Jacob was by now on his second
marriage, and his mother Amalie was twenty

00:02:38.939 --> 00:02:40.510
years Jacob’s junior.

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:45.070
One consequence of this was that, by the time
Freud was born, he already had a nephew a

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:46.070
year older than him.

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:47.920
John was Freud’s first great friendship.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:49.849
As toddlers, the two were inseparable.

00:02:49.849 --> 00:02:53.180
But, as Freud would later remark, they were
also rivals.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:57.530
That model of an intimate male friendship
that was also overshadowed by great rivalry

00:02:57.530 --> 00:03:00.440
would be one that repeated throughout Freud’s
life.

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:05.500
In 1859, Freud’s older half-brother took
John and moved away to Manchester in England.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:09.970
At first, Freud tried to fill the hole of
John’s frenemyship by becoming unhealthily

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:15.390
close to his mother, but while Amalie doted
on her boy, she was also by now heavily pregnant.

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:20.030
Needless to say, the subsequent glut of four
girls and two boys vying for Amalie’s attention

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:22.599
would make young Freud crazy jealous.

00:03:22.599 --> 00:03:27.689
That same year, 1859, Jacob’s business collapsed
and he moved the family to Leipzig.

00:03:27.689 --> 00:03:32.409
But things were no better in Saxony and, in
1860, the Freuds joined the mass influx of

00:03:32.409 --> 00:03:36.019
Jews from across the Austrian Empire moving
to Vienna.

00:03:36.019 --> 00:03:41.200
The austere capital of a deeply conservative
monarchy, Vienna in 1860 was a European power

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:48.319
center, but one with a dull reputation; the
dowdy sister of elegant Paris and industrious

00:03:48.319 --> 00:03:49.319
London.

00:03:49.319 --> 00:03:51.630
Yet it was also a capital on the cusp of change.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:56.590
As Jewish intellectuals, Hungarian aristocrats,
and German artists all converged on the city,

00:03:56.590 --> 00:03:59.480
the seeds were planted for Vienna’s transformation.

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:03.219
One of the key figures in this transformation
would be Sigmund Freud.

00:04:03.219 --> 00:04:06.270
The family’s early years in Vienna aren’t
much to remark upon.

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:10.319
They settled into Leopoldstadt and Jacob presumably
got a decent job.

00:04:10.319 --> 00:04:15.140
We can infer this because, in 1865, he was
able to pay for 9-year old Freud to attend

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:16.580
an excellent gymnasium.

00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:22.110
And, no, not that sort of gymnasium; “gymnasium”
is the Central European term for a specialist

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:23.110
school.

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:27.110
During his years at this Lycra-less gymnasium,
Freud excelled at academic subjects, developed

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:32.280
a voracious appetite for books, and had yet
another of his trademark intimate frenemyships,

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:34.300
this time with a lad named Eduard.

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:38.750
But it wasn’t until his last year there
that Freud made a casual decision that would

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:39.750
change his life.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:44.660
A public reading of one of Goethe’s nature
essays was being staged, and the young Freud

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:45.890
went along on a whim.

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:49.890
It was while listening to this essay that
Freud had a flash of inspiration.

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:52.480
He now knew what he wanted to do with his
life.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:56.840
He was going to study medicine.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:05:02.810
Making the Man
The next decade passed in a blur.

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:06.660
In 1873, Freud enrolled at the University
of Vienna as a medical student, where he gained

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:09.040
a solid reputation as a brain anatomist.

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:13.190
It was while at the University that he was
introduced to the older Dr Josef Breuer, a

00:05:13.190 --> 00:05:16.390
guy who is gonna become really important to
our story really soon.

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:20.640
Two years later, in 1875, Freud traveled to
England for the first time, meeting up with

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:22.000
his old frenemy John.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.941
He returned to Vienna obsessed with Britain,
despite, as he put it, all the “fog and

00:05:25.941 --> 00:05:28.560
rain, drunkenness and conservatism.”

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:32.080
By 1881, Freud was a qualified medical practitioner.

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:35.620
Barely was the ink dry on his degree than
he’d met and fallen in love with his future

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:36.620
wife, Martha Bernays.

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:38.620
See what we mean?

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:39.780
Busy decade.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:43.160
Bernays and Freud were soon engaged, but there
was a problem.

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:45.410
Martha Bernays came from a highly respected
family.

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:48.120
Her grandfather had been chief Rabbi of Hamburg.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:50.700
Her parents were used to the finer things.

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:54.061
So when this semi-impoverished wannabee doctor
came and asked for their daughter’s hand

00:05:54.061 --> 00:05:56.820
in marriage, they were all like:
“Uh, no.

00:05:56.820 --> 00:06:00.510
Come back when you’ve got a decent job and
aren’t dressed like a hobo.”

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:05.700
And so Freud found himself, in 1882, suddenly
in desperate need of a steady income.

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:10.180
For a Viennese medical student at that time,
the best way to get ahead was to do something

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:15.690
groundbreaking, get noticed, and use your
fame to set up a practice.

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:20.180
While Freud already had a reputation as a
brain expert, he had nothing on his CV guaranteed

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:21.350
to make his name.

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:25.290
So Freud decided to roll everything on a gamble
that couldn’t fail.

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:28.800
He was going to introduce Vienna to cocaine.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:38.120
We promise we haven’t got confused and accidentally
started reading from our old Pablo Escobar

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:39.400
script.

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:44.570
Freud really was certain that a breakthrough
with cocaine was where his future fame – and

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:46.930
the money to marry Martha – lay.

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:51.560
In April, 1884, he brought a huge quantity
and began experimenting with it as a cure

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:56.130
for depression, for indigestion, for alcoholism…
anything that might work.

00:06:56.130 --> 00:06:59.620
Unfortunately, he missed the one major breakthrough
cocaine could have afforded him.

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:03.310
It was Freud’s friend, Karl Koller, who
discovered it could be used to numb people’s

00:07:03.310 --> 00:07:04.560
eyes prior to surgery.

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:08.280
By the time a year had passed, Freud’s great
foray into cocaine had gotten him nothing

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:11.340
but missed opportunities and an expensive
addiction.

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:13.630
So he decided to change tack.

00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:17.460
That July, Freud left Vienna and traveled
to Paris to study under the great neurologist

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:18.460
Jean-Martin Charcot.

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:23.710
For most of the 19th Century, nervous disorders
had been approached from a biological perspective,

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:25.690
like fixing a leaky ship.

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:29.370
If you could discover where the fault was
and patch it, you’d have a whole person

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:30.370
again.

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:31.370
But Jean-Martin Charcot had changed that.

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:34.090
Where others would talk about surgery, Charcot
was interested in hypnosis.

00:07:34.090 --> 00:07:38.850
Where doctors in Vienna might whisper about
brain structure, Charcot was dabbling with

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:42.280
healing patients using only the power of words.

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:47.530
For Freud, witnessing Charcot’s methods
was more mind-blowing than a noseful of Uncle

00:07:47.530 --> 00:07:49.560
Escobar’s marching powder.

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:53.290
Freud returned to Vienna in 1886, certain
Charcot’s methods would give him an edge

00:07:53.290 --> 00:07:54.460
in his competitive field.

00:07:54.460 --> 00:07:56.550
At first it looked like another fail.

00:07:56.550 --> 00:08:01.460
Freud’s contemporaries basically accused
him of falling for a load of French merde.

00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:03.380
But Freud was determined.

00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:07.440
He set up his own practice, consulting on
nervous diseases using hypnosis.

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:10.740
And he soon had a regular enough income to
be able to afford marriage.

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:14.780
On September 13, 1886, Sigmund Freud and Martha
Bernays were wed.

00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:18.290
Over the next eight years, their union would
produce six children.

00:08:18.290 --> 00:08:22.520
For Freud, marrying Martha must’ve felt
like the affirmation he’d been waiting for.

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:27.360
Here he was, after all those false starts,
owner of a successful practice, and married

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:28.420
to the woman of his dreams.

00:08:28.420 --> 00:08:31.460
But Freud wasn’t content to just stop now.

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:36.579
He’d gotten a taste for grandiose ideas,
for eccentric innovations.

00:08:36.579 --> 00:08:40.310
His next one would change medicine forever.

00:08:40.310 --> 00:08:47.889
A Brand New Science
Come the mid-1890s, Freud was restless.

00:08:47.889 --> 00:08:51.879
He’d been practicing his brand of psychotherapy
for nearly ten years now, and was no longer

00:08:51.879 --> 00:08:53.550
getting the results he desired.

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:57.861
It was while in this despondent mood that
he got to talking with his old teacher, Dr

00:08:57.861 --> 00:08:59.380
Josef Breuer.

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:03.110
During the conversation, Breuer just happened
to mention one of his own old cases.

00:09:03.110 --> 00:09:06.720
Way back in 1882, Breuer had treated a woman
he called Anna O.

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:11.399
Anna had been suffering from what used to
be called hysteria, a catch all term 19th

00:09:11.399 --> 00:09:17.029
Century doctors used to mean anything from
seizures, to anxiety, to being too unladylike.

00:09:17.029 --> 00:09:18.939
But Anna had also been strong willed.

00:09:18.939 --> 00:09:23.470
She shrugged off Breuer’s attempts to use
hypnosis, and suggested they work via talking.

00:09:23.470 --> 00:09:27.959
Over the next few weeks, patient and doctor
developed something they called the “talking

00:09:27.959 --> 00:09:28.959
cure”.

00:09:28.959 --> 00:09:33.810
Instead of Breuer using hypnosis to implant
suggestions, he’d just allowed Anna to unburden

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:36.410
herself, a kind of cathartic release.

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:41.589
The idea that you could use talking to get
at hidden trauma was the craziest thing Freud

00:09:41.589 --> 00:09:44.660
had heard since his days with Charcot.

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:46.870
Naturally, he loved it.

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:52.750
In 1895 he helped Breuer write a case study
they published together as Studien über Hysterie.

00:09:52.750 --> 00:09:56.910
However, Breuer was uncomfortable with Freud’s
obsession with his case.

00:09:56.910 --> 00:09:59.949
Despite what he claimed, Breuer hadn’t actually
cured Anna.

00:09:59.949 --> 00:10:04.089
She was still in decline, and today it’s
thought unlikely Breuer’s treatment was

00:10:04.089 --> 00:10:05.089
helpful.

00:10:05.089 --> 00:10:06.089
Still, Freud persisted.

00:10:06.089 --> 00:10:08.970
He began introducing the talking method to
his own practice.

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:13.829
Invented the classic psychoanalyst set-up
we know from movies: y’know, with the patient

00:10:13.829 --> 00:10:17.680
lying on a comfortable couch, while the doctor
sits out of sight, taking notes.

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:22.200
Pretty soon, Freud was using free association
to get his bourgeois patients to talk about

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:23.889
their darkest desires.

00:10:23.889 --> 00:10:26.769
And he kept finding out that all of them were
obsessed with sex.

00:10:26.769 --> 00:10:31.210
So, this is where we get to the first of many
controversial parts of Freud’s work.

00:10:31.210 --> 00:10:35.160
All of Freud’s patients were female, and
it’s a frequent accusation that Freud was

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:38.449
simply pathologizing ordinary female sexuality.

00:10:38.449 --> 00:10:42.589
Whether or not that’s true, Freud soon came
to believe that our hidden desires were at

00:10:42.589 --> 00:10:44.529
the root of many of our problems.

00:10:44.529 --> 00:10:49.639
Before long, he’d coined a term for the
process of treating these problems: psychoanalysis.

00:10:49.639 --> 00:10:53.959
But if you’re expecting to hear this is
the beginning of Freud’s rise to becoming

00:10:53.959 --> 00:10:56.829
a household name, you may have to think again.

00:10:56.829 --> 00:11:00.720
For Freud, the late 1890s were spent in the
intellectual wilderness.

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:02.180
Sure, things were OK financially.

00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:04.399
And his home life was going fine.

00:11:04.399 --> 00:11:08.320
Martha had just given birth to their youngest
daughter – the future child therapist Anna

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:09.320
Freud.

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:11.399
But Freud’s reputation was floundering.

00:11:11.399 --> 00:11:15.350
His initial attempts to create a handbook
for psychoanalysis collapsed.

00:11:15.350 --> 00:11:19.660
Among the Catholic establishment, there were
rumblings that Freud was wasting his time

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:21.149
with some “Jewish science”.

00:11:21.149 --> 00:11:25.259
Once again, Freud was in need of a breakthrough
to put his name in the spotlight.

00:11:25.259 --> 00:11:27.519
In 1897, he found it.

00:11:27.519 --> 00:11:31.569
Up until that point, Freud had listened as
many of his patients described what appeared

00:11:31.569 --> 00:11:34.250
to be sexual abuse in their childhoods.

00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:37.199
Before 1897, Freud had tended to believe them.

00:11:37.199 --> 00:11:39.350
That year, he changed tack.

00:11:39.350 --> 00:11:44.480
Freud’s eureka moment was deciding the abuse
his patients reported wasn’t real.

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:50.160
Instead, it was all fantasies brought on by
repressing their sexuality in childhood.

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:53.569
And here we get to yet another controversial
segment.

00:11:53.569 --> 00:11:57.829
Nowadays, it’s acknowledged that children
experience immature sexual feelings, usually

00:11:57.829 --> 00:12:02.959
in the form of embarrassing crushes or really,
really wanting to hold Chloe’s hand during

00:12:02.959 --> 00:12:03.959
recess.

00:12:03.959 --> 00:12:07.319
But we also acknowledge that some adults really
do abuse children.

00:12:07.319 --> 00:12:09.660
Even adults from respectable, middle class
families.

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:14.709
It’s far from certain, but some argue Freud
unwittingly helped cover up a culture of abuse

00:12:14.709 --> 00:12:15.709
in Vienna.

00:12:15.709 --> 00:12:19.899
Still, the breakthrough gave Freud the notoriety
he’d always craved.

00:12:19.899 --> 00:12:25.009
Now he just had to convince the deeply conservative
society he lived in to take his wild thoughts

00:12:25.009 --> 00:12:28.450
on sexuality seriously.

00:12:28.450 --> 00:13:24.430
How hard could

00:13:24.430 --> 00:13:27.870
that be?

00:13:27.870 --> 00:13:55.529
Breakthrough
If the 1890s were Freud’s wilderness years,

00:13:55.529 --> 00:13:59.950
the first decade of the 20th Century was Freud
marching in from the desert, kicking down

00:13:59.950 --> 00:14:03.790
the gates of Vienna, and forcing the world
to listen to his ideas.

00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:06.760
In 1900, Die Traumdeutung – know in English
as The Interpretation of Dreams – hit the

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:09.870
bookshelves with all the force of a bomb going
off.

00:14:09.870 --> 00:14:14.269
The basic outline of Freud’s thesis was
linking dreams with repressed desire, often

00:14:14.269 --> 00:14:19.930
stemming from childhood, while also talking
about fantasy and daydreams reflecting unconscious

00:14:19.930 --> 00:14:20.930
wants.

00:14:20.930 --> 00:14:23.910
If that sounds a little abstract, know that
this book is where the idea of the Oedipus

00:14:23.910 --> 00:14:27.820
Complex comes from, something that we still
talk about over a century later.

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:32.389
But the most consequential part was Freud’s
insistence that his discoveries didn’t just

00:14:32.389 --> 00:14:33.720
apply to his patients.

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:35.270
They were universal.

00:14:35.270 --> 00:14:39.920
This great ocean of dark desire was roiling
away in all of us.

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:43.779
It was this insight that would ensure Freud’s
name went down in history.

00:14:43.779 --> 00:14:48.529
Starting in 1902, Freud followed up Die Traumdeutung
with regular lectures at Vienna University.

00:14:48.529 --> 00:14:52.629
Although the number of attendees was small,
there was a core, loyal group.

00:14:52.629 --> 00:14:57.180
Before long, that group was meeting with Freud
every Wednesday to discuss psychoanalysis.

00:14:57.180 --> 00:15:02.470
By 1908 they would grow into the hugely influential
Vienna Psychoanalytical Society.

00:15:02.470 --> 00:15:06.720
As these meetings began to spread Freud’s
ideas, the man himself was hard at work, churning

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:08.360
out book after book.

00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:10.970
First came Zur Psychopathologie des Alltagslebens
(The Pathology of Everyday Life), which introduced

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:15.630
the world to Freudian slips – disappointingly
not a lacy undergarment you wear to seduce

00:15:15.630 --> 00:15:19.559
your mother, but the act of accidentally saying
something revealing.

00:15:19.559 --> 00:15:24.819
This was followed in 1905 by Fragment of an
Analysis of a Case of Hysteria, which first

00:15:24.819 --> 00:15:27.300
diagnosed the phenomenon of transference.

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:31.749
It was around this time that Freud’s growing
fame brought him into contact with Carl Jung.

00:15:31.749 --> 00:15:36.139
To say Jung was important to Freud’s life
is like saying the Civil War probably affected

00:15:36.139 --> 00:15:37.600
Abraham Lincoln’s career.

00:15:37.600 --> 00:15:40.769
Their collaboration was intense and tragic.

00:15:40.769 --> 00:15:44.550
Intense because their meetings would turn
into nonstop 13 hour debates.

00:15:44.550 --> 00:15:47.689
And tragic because… well, you’ll see.

00:15:47.689 --> 00:15:51.209
In Jung, Freud saw someone who could make
psychoanalysis mainstream.

00:15:51.209 --> 00:15:55.319
Both because Jung was twenty years younger,
but also because he was a gentile.

00:15:55.319 --> 00:15:59.069
He may have been an atheist all his life,
but Freud was only too aware of how Europe

00:15:59.069 --> 00:16:01.040
saw even nonobservant Jews.

00:16:01.040 --> 00:16:05.019
That his discoveries could be pigeonholed
as mere Jewish quackery.

00:16:05.019 --> 00:16:09.529
For several years, the collaboration spurred
both Freud and Jung to greater heights.

00:16:09.529 --> 00:16:13.589
Jung was there when Freud held the first international
psychoanalytic congress in Salzburg.

00:16:13.589 --> 00:16:18.309
He was there when Freud gave his only lecture
series in America, at Clark University, Massachusetts.

00:16:18.309 --> 00:16:23.220
And he was there, defending Freud in the public
eye whenever his ideas were ridiculed.

00:16:23.220 --> 00:16:25.149
Alas, it was not to last.

00:16:25.149 --> 00:16:30.499
In 1913, resentments that had been building
between the two men finally came exploding

00:16:30.499 --> 00:16:31.499
out.

00:16:31.499 --> 00:16:33.930
There are many theories about what caused
their rift, but given today’s subject, it

00:16:33.930 --> 00:16:37.869
seems only fitting to highlight one particularly
Freudian interpretation.

00:16:37.869 --> 00:16:42.610
We know that Freud occasionally fretted he
had a gay side – indeed, he claimed all

00:16:42.610 --> 00:16:47.019
humans were innately bisexual – and that
he may have harbored repressed feelings for

00:16:47.019 --> 00:16:48.019
Jung.

00:16:48.019 --> 00:16:52.019
We also know that Jung had unresolved anger
issues relating to his father, feelings he

00:16:52.019 --> 00:16:54.769
often directed towards father figures like
Freud.

00:16:54.769 --> 00:16:59.019
Whatever the truth, the break between the
two men was permanent.

00:16:59.019 --> 00:17:03.050
In 1913, Freud wrote the younger man a letter,
telling him he never wanted to see him again.

00:17:03.050 --> 00:17:04.510
Jung seems to have agreed.

00:17:04.510 --> 00:17:06.610
He never tried to contact Freud again.

00:17:06.610 --> 00:17:11.670
It was the end of a period of intense creativity
for Freud, and the end of his dreams of grooming

00:17:11.670 --> 00:17:12.700
a successor.

00:17:12.700 --> 00:17:16.570
But that time period was also the end of something
else, too.

00:17:16.570 --> 00:17:26.209
Freud didn’t know it, but he was living
through the last days of Europe.

00:17:26.209 --> 00:17:27.930
The End of a World
The First World War was like a wrecking ball

00:17:27.930 --> 00:17:30.410
crashing into the house of cards marked ‘history’.

00:17:30.410 --> 00:17:35.800
In the short years between 1914 and 1918,
the war swept away three whole empires, including

00:17:35.800 --> 00:17:37.040
Austria-Hungary.

00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:41.390
Come late 1918, Vienna had gone from being
a seat of European power to the backwater

00:17:41.390 --> 00:17:44.840
capital of a small, impoverished nation known
simply as Austria.

00:17:44.840 --> 00:17:48.670
For Freud, the war had been marked by the
constant threat of tragedy, both personal

00:17:48.670 --> 00:17:49.670
and professional.

00:17:49.670 --> 00:17:53.620
On the personal side, all three of his sons
had been drafted to serve in the Austro-Hungarian

00:17:53.620 --> 00:17:57.300
Army, making the death of at least one of
them seem almost inevitable.

00:17:57.300 --> 00:18:00.820
On the professional side, the outbreak of
war killed the international psychoanalysis

00:18:00.820 --> 00:18:02.060
movement stone dead.

00:18:02.060 --> 00:18:06.930
For four long years, Freud was confined to
Vienna, able to do nothing but write, think,

00:18:06.930 --> 00:18:08.390
and dream of a better future.

00:18:08.390 --> 00:18:12.750
It was during this period that the lifelong
cigar smoker first discovered a strange growth

00:18:12.750 --> 00:18:13.990
on his jaw.

00:18:13.990 --> 00:18:15.170
Yet Freud was lucky.

00:18:15.170 --> 00:18:17.670
All three of his sons came back alive from
the front.

00:18:17.670 --> 00:18:22.810
And when the war ended in 1918 and the economy
of Austria was battered by runaway inflation,

00:18:22.810 --> 00:18:27.080
Freud was able to take British clients to
provide his family with hard currency.

00:18:27.080 --> 00:18:30.340
For the lifelong anglophile Freud, this was
a dream come true.

00:18:30.340 --> 00:18:34.290
His patients were from the famous Bloomsbury
set, a collection of freethinking writers

00:18:34.290 --> 00:18:35.290
and artists.

00:18:35.290 --> 00:18:38.460
When they came to him for treatment, Freud
made it a condition that they translate his

00:18:38.460 --> 00:18:39.460
works into English.

00:18:39.460 --> 00:18:43.320
It was this agreement that would eventually
make Freud a household name.

00:18:43.320 --> 00:18:47.350
Yet it was also in this postwar period that
tragedy finally struck.

00:18:47.350 --> 00:18:51.300
In 1919, a virulent new strain of influenza
swept the world.

00:18:51.300 --> 00:18:55.130
Known as Spanish Flu, it remains one of the
deadliest pandemics in history.

00:18:55.130 --> 00:18:59.290
In one short year, it killed up to 50 million
people.

00:18:59.290 --> 00:19:02.010
Among that 50 million was Freud’s daughter,
Sophie.

00:19:02.010 --> 00:19:05.340
The death of Sophie came as a devastating
blow to Freud.

00:19:05.340 --> 00:19:09.630
Barely had he recovered than he himself was
diagnosed with cancer of the jaw.

00:19:09.630 --> 00:19:13.360
It was the start of a long decline from which
Freud would never recover.

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:14.750
But life goes on.

00:19:14.750 --> 00:19:18.710
As the Great War gave way to the Roaring Twenties,
Freud continued to write, publishing Das Ich

00:19:18.710 --> 00:19:23.010
und das Es (The Ego and the Id), the work
that gave us terms like superego that are

00:19:23.010 --> 00:19:24.210
still in use today.

00:19:24.210 --> 00:19:29.010
He also began to focus on his daughter Anna’s
development, taking her to psychiatric conferences,

00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:33.000
and increasingly sending her in his place
when the cancer left him too weak.

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:36.950
It was around this time, when Freud was at
his lowest ebb, that psychoanalysis really

00:19:36.950 --> 00:19:38.110
began to take off.

00:19:38.110 --> 00:19:43.060
In Paris, the surrealists founded an entire
art movement inspired by Freud’s discoveries.

00:19:43.060 --> 00:19:47.710
In Vienna, Freud found himself suddenly treating
the fabulously wealthy, like Marie Bonaparte,

00:19:47.710 --> 00:19:49.340
a descendant of Napoleon.

00:19:49.340 --> 00:19:52.630
By the end of the twenties, you could even
convince yourself Freud’s life was getting

00:19:52.630 --> 00:19:53.690
back on track.

00:19:53.690 --> 00:19:55.090
The cancer still hadn’t killed him.

00:19:55.090 --> 00:19:59.550
He was publishing books like Civilization
and its Discontents, and psychoanalysis was

00:19:59.550 --> 00:20:04.250
taking off in America
But sadly, we already know this was just an

00:20:04.250 --> 00:20:05.250
illusion.

00:20:05.250 --> 00:20:08.980
From our vantage point of the 21st Century,
we can already see the clouds gathering on

00:20:08.980 --> 00:20:09.980
the horizon.

00:20:09.980 --> 00:20:15.180
The storm that will be unleashed by one nationalist
with a fanatical hatred for Jews.

00:20:15.180 --> 00:20:20.940
For the Jewish Freud, still living in Vienna,
that storm would be destined to consume him.

00:20:20.940 --> 00:20:28.870
Burning Books, Burning People
On May 10, 1933, a large group of students

00:20:28.870 --> 00:20:31.330
gathered outside the State Opera in Berlin.

00:20:31.330 --> 00:20:34.280
But these students weren’t there to down
kegs or party.

00:20:34.280 --> 00:20:36.760
They were there to burn books.

00:20:36.760 --> 00:20:40.780
Barely two months earlier, the German parliament
had passed the Enabling Act, handing total

00:20:40.780 --> 00:20:42.260
power to Adolf Hitler.

00:20:42.260 --> 00:20:46.810
Now the Nazi student groups were showing their
enthusiasm for the new regime by consigning

00:20:46.810 --> 00:20:50.040
25,000 “un-German” books to the flames.

00:20:50.040 --> 00:20:53.340
Among the books that burned that day were
the works of Sigmund Freud.

00:20:53.340 --> 00:20:57.510
In Vienna, Freud could only watch as neighboring
Germany fell to Nazism.

00:20:57.510 --> 00:21:01.330
His sole consolation may have been that Austria
hadn’t yet succumbed.

00:21:01.330 --> 00:21:06.380
Although fascists seized control of the country
in 1934, they stopped short of the outright

00:21:06.380 --> 00:21:07.840
anti-Semitism of the Nazis.

00:21:07.840 --> 00:21:11.680
Still, the growing violence in Germany must’ve
put everyone on edge.

00:21:11.680 --> 00:21:16.370
What if Hitler decided to take over Austria?

00:21:16.370 --> 00:21:18.160
In 1938, he finally did.

00:21:18.160 --> 00:21:23.080
On March 12, German tanks rolled across the
border, annexing Austria into the Third Reich.

00:21:23.080 --> 00:21:26.100
On the streets of Vienna, people lined up
to cheer the Nazis.

00:21:26.100 --> 00:21:29.070
Buildings were draped in Swastikas, street
parties held.

00:21:29.070 --> 00:21:34.820
All alone in his apartment, Freud simple noted
two words in his diary: 'Finis Austriae'.

00:21:34.820 --> 00:21:39.300
By this stage, Freud was nearly dead from
the cancer spread throughout his system; a

00:21:39.300 --> 00:21:43.430
walking cadaver that couldn’t even make
it to the family’s summerhouse outside Vienna.

00:21:43.430 --> 00:21:47.250
When people suggested he leave the country
he told them it would never happen.

00:21:47.250 --> 00:21:50.140
Nor would it have, had it not been for what
happened to Anna.

00:21:50.140 --> 00:21:53.440
On March 22, Anna Freud was arrested by the
Gestapo.

00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:57.820
They ruthlessly interrogated her for hours
before throwing her back out onto the street.

00:21:57.820 --> 00:22:00.390
The incident shook the elderly Freud to his
core.

00:22:00.390 --> 00:22:02.720
The next day, he decided to leave Austria.

00:22:02.720 --> 00:22:04.310
This was easier said than done.

00:22:04.310 --> 00:22:08.090
Under the new Nazi regime, the whereabouts
of Jews was closely tracked.

00:22:08.090 --> 00:22:11.790
Leaving the country required a special exit
visa, and you can guess how often the Gestapo

00:22:11.790 --> 00:22:13.000
handed those out.

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:15.920
Thankfully, Freud had three very important
people on his side.

00:22:15.920 --> 00:22:18.200
The first was his patient, Marie Bonaparte.

00:22:18.200 --> 00:22:21.050
The second was William Bullitt, the US ambassador
in Vienna.

00:22:21.050 --> 00:22:23.330
But the third was less expected.

00:22:23.330 --> 00:22:28.250
Komissar Anton Sauerwald was a dyed in the
wool Nazi, tasked with overseeing Jews in

00:22:28.250 --> 00:22:29.250
Vienna.

00:22:29.250 --> 00:22:32.600
But Sauerwald was also a former student of
one of Freud’s closest friends.

00:22:32.600 --> 00:22:36.390
He’d spent all his years of study learning
to respect the famous doctor.

00:22:36.390 --> 00:22:40.040
So when he discovered Freud was looking for
an out, he decided to help him.

00:22:40.040 --> 00:22:44.310
That Spring, Sauerwald secured exit visas
for Freud and 16 of his family members.

00:22:44.310 --> 00:22:48.940
Elsewhere, Princess Marie was using her vast
fortune to grease as many palms as possible,

00:22:48.940 --> 00:22:51.920
while William Bullitt was applying diplomatic
pressure.

00:22:51.920 --> 00:22:56.060
After three months, the Freuds were finally
given the all clear to leave Austria for England.

00:22:56.060 --> 00:22:59.230
The ride to London must’ve been both hopeful
and heartbreaking.

00:22:59.230 --> 00:23:03.990
Here was Freud, finally fulfilling his teenage
dream to live in England, but under the gloomiest

00:23:03.990 --> 00:23:04.990
circumstances.

00:23:04.990 --> 00:23:08.820
It probably didn’t help that all four of
Freud’s elderly sisters had been unable

00:23:08.820 --> 00:23:09.980
to get exit visas.

00:23:09.980 --> 00:23:13.800
Although Freud wouldn’t live long enough
to hear the news, all four would perish in

00:23:13.800 --> 00:23:15.190
concentration camps.

00:23:15.190 --> 00:23:20.380
On June 6, 1938, Freud and his family finally
arrived in London by train.

00:23:20.380 --> 00:23:25.100
Word had leaked out, and a huge crowd had
gathered to welcome the world’s most famous

00:23:25.100 --> 00:23:26.100
psychoanalyst.

00:23:26.100 --> 00:23:30.960
As he saw the rows upon rows of people cheering,
Freud felt like he’d finally come home.

00:23:30.960 --> 00:23:33.190
*
Freud would live barely a year after reaching

00:23:33.190 --> 00:23:38.370
England, passing away on September 23, 1939,
just twenty days after Britain declared war

00:23:38.370 --> 00:23:39.370
on Germany.

00:23:39.370 --> 00:23:41.920
He kept receiving patients almost until the
very end.

00:23:41.920 --> 00:23:46.360
When the author H.G Wells came to visit, Freud
confided in him that it had always been a

00:23:46.360 --> 00:23:48.300
fantasy of his to be an Englishman.

00:23:48.300 --> 00:23:51.700
In its own strange way, that fantasy had come
true.

00:23:51.700 --> 00:23:56.140
Today, it’s fashionable to knock Freud’s
theories, to poke fun at his experiments with

00:23:56.140 --> 00:23:58.770
cocaine, his obsession with sex.

00:23:58.770 --> 00:24:03.690
Certainly, there are few psychiatrists today
who would say Freud’s methods were completely

00:24:03.690 --> 00:24:04.690
sound.

00:24:04.690 --> 00:24:07.470
Yet to just focus on Freud’s medical work
is to miss the point.

00:24:07.470 --> 00:24:12.090
No matter what you think of him, Freud completely
transformed the way we understood the human

00:24:12.090 --> 00:24:13.090
mind.

00:24:13.090 --> 00:24:16.620
Just think how often you hear about people
having “complexes”, or suffering from

00:24:16.620 --> 00:24:20.600
transference, or having problems with mother
or father figures.

00:24:20.600 --> 00:24:25.220
Think how deeply ideas of psychoanalysis and
childhood trauma have penetrated into our

00:24:25.220 --> 00:24:27.210
fiction, into our art.

00:24:27.210 --> 00:24:30.510
Think just of how significant surrealism became.

00:24:30.510 --> 00:24:33.730
Sigmund Freud may have died an exile, unloved
by his home country.

00:24:33.730 --> 00:24:39.970
But he also died the father of psychoanalysis,
and the greatest explorer of the human mind.

00:24:39.970 --> 00:25:00.400
For that reason, if no other, he deserves
to be remembered.

